sections.method:
The paper discusses related work in three main areas. First, Few-shot Image Manipulation: Existing methods mostly rely on diffusion models and ControlNet, sometimes freezing pre-trained models and finetuning condition tokens or learning representations of keys and values. However, these models are generally weak in reasoning compared to autoregressive models. Second, Visual In-context Learning: ICL has been observed in LLMs and extended to vision tasks like segmentation, scene understanding, and 3D modeling. Recent unified autoregressive models like Emu2 and Codi-2 show strong ICL in generation, but few-shot image manipulation remains under-explored. Third, Autoregressive Models for Image Generation: There is growing interest in LLMs that can directly process and generate image tokens (e.g., Emu, SEED, SEED-X). InstaManip builds on these architectures to specifically address the challenges of few-shot image manipulation. The problem is defined as learning a distribution of a manipulated image $\mathcal{Y}$ given a query image $\mathcal{X}$, a textual instruction $\mathcal{T}$, and exemplar pairs $(\mathcal{X}', \mathcal{Y}')$: 
\begin{equation} P(\mathcal {Y}|\mathcal {X},\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}') \end{equation}
Inspired by human cognition, this is decomposed into two stages using a latent variable $\mathcal{Z}$ for abstract manipulation features:
\begin{equation} P(\mathcal {Y}|\mathcal {X},\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}') = \underbrace {P(\mathcal {Z}|\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}')}_{\circled {1}} \cdot \underbrace {P(\mathcal {Y}|\mathcal {X},\mathcal {Z})}_{\circled {2}} \end{equation}
where $\circled {1}$ is the learning stage and $\circled {2}$ is the applying stage. 

**Prompt Composition**: Images are tokenized into 64 continuous visual tokens using a pre-trained encoder and aligned via a linear layer. $M$ learnable manipulation tokens $\mathcal{Z}$ are initialized from word embeddings. The prompt follows a template: "Here is an image manipulation instruction {textual instruction}, which can edit source image {exemplar source image} to target image {exemplar target image}. The editing is embedded in {manipulation tokens}. Learn from the instruction with the exemplar pairs and apply the same manipulation to this image {query image}." 64 generation tokens are added at the end.

**Group Self-Attention**: The input is split into two groups. Group 1 ($\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}', \mathcal{Y}', \mathcal{Z}$) handles the learning stage:
\begin{equation} \footnotesize GSA_1 = \sigma \left (\frac {\bm {Q}_{\left [\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}',\mathcal {Z}\right ]} \bm {K}_{\left [\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}',\mathcal {Z}\right ]}^T - \bm {S}_1}{\sqrt {D}}\right ) \cdot \bm {V}_{\left [\mathcal {T},\mathcal {X}',\mathcal {Y}',\mathcal {Z}\right ]} \end{equation}
Group 2 ($\mathcal{Z}, \mathcal{X}, \tilde{\mathcal{Y}}$) handles the applying stage:
\begin{equation} \footnotesize GSA_2 = \sigma \left (\frac {\bm {Q}_{\left [\mathcal {Z},\mathcal {X},\mathcal {\tilde {Y}}\right ]} \bm {K}_{\left [\mathcal {Z},\mathcal {X},\mathcal {\tilde {Y}}\right ]}^T - \bm {S}_2}{\sqrt {D}}\right ) \cdot \bm {V}_{\left [\mathcal {Z},\mathcal {X},\mathcal {\tilde {Y}}\right ]} \end{equation}
$\mathcal{Z}$ serves as the only bridge between the groups, enforcing the learning of transferable features.

**Relation Regularization**: To ensure $\mathcal{Z}$ learns relevant features, the model enforces that semantically similar instructions have close manipulation embeddings. For a batch $B$, averaged manipulation tokens $\bar{Z}_i$ are compared to CLIP-encoded textual instruction similarities:
\begin{equation} \mathcal {L}_{relation} = \frac {1}{N} \sum _{i=1}^N \left \|\mathcal {\bar {Z}}_i\mathcal {\bar {Z}}_i^T- \phi (\mathcal {T})\phi (\mathcal {T})^T\right \|_F^2 \end{equation}

**Implementation Details**: The model uses LLaMA2-13B (40 layers), Qwen ViT as the image encoder, and SDXL as the decoder. $M=30$ manipulation tokens are used. Training involves freezing the encoder/decoder and optimizing via LoRA with $\alpha=0.1$. Total loss is:
\begin{equation} \mathcal {L} = \mathcal {L}_{recon} + \alpha \mathcal {L}_{relation} \end{equation}
